                <p class="dateAesteticTitle2 center">Entre les mots by Farid Ismail</p>
            </div>
            <div class="col s12 m2"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col s12 m2"></div>
            <div class="col s12 m8 paragraphStyleNews">
                <p class="bolderFont">
                    In an intimate conversation with Vilpa Max, filmmaker Farid Ismail reflects on stuttering,
                    perseverance, and the power of cinema to tell stories that words alone cannot express.
                </p>
                <p>
                    For director <span class="bolderFont">Farid Ismail, </span> Between the Words (Entre les mots) is
                    more than a short film. It's a deeply
                    personal exploration of communication, vulnerability, and the universal desire to be heard.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The comedy-drama follows Frédéric, a law student who stutters and must confront one of his greatest
                    fears: an oral examination. Drawing from his own experiences, Ismail transforms an ordinary academic
                    challenge into an emotional journey.
                </p>
                <p>
                    "I've always found a film about stuttering very cinematic, because you can express a situation
                    through images rather than words," Ismail explains.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Authenticity was essential to bringing Frédéric to life. Working closely with actor Jonathan
                    Bartholmé, Ismail extended rehearsals to two months, ensuring the performance would go beyond
                    stereotypes and portray a fully realized character.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The film also embraces humor, a deliberate choice by Ismail to make difficult subjects more
                    approachable. For him, comedy creates a connection with audiences while addressing experiences such
                    as bullying, social pressure, and the frustration of not being understood.
                </p>
                <p>At its heart, Entre les mots challenges a society increasingly obsessed with speed. Ismail believes
                    that people who communicate differently deserve patience, respect, and the opportunity to express
                    themselves without interruption.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Following its journey through international film festivals, the director was surprised by how widely
                    audiences connected with the story, including people who had never experienced stuttering themselves
                </p>
                <p>For Ismail, that connection is what makes storytelling meaningful. And although the film's
                    recognition has strengthened his confidence as a filmmaker, his greatest achievement remains
                    something much simpler: bringing a deeply personal story to the screen.</p>
                <p class="bolderFont">
                    As he puts it, "the real award is finishing a movie."
                </p>
